{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-340712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{11}=\\frac{11}{s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042893/state.json" "file_sha256" "caddfd3715359a191831784912dca6bce792c61a21c4f35b5027000a8e5305cd"} {"question" "q-340712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11$ is $F(s)=\\frac{11}{s},$ defined for $s>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042893/state.json" "file_sha256" "caddfd3715359a191831784912dca6bce792c61a21c4f35b5027000a8e5305cd"} {"question" "q-340712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042893/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d0b570eda3f9b59381be1afdf44aa0f5f11c8c6a23ee6730025f33449cb24786"} {"question" "q-340712" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/q