exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{{}_{0}}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{7}{3s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3s}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{3s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-\\frac{7}{3}$ is $F(s)=-\\frac{7}{3s},$ defined for $s>0.$" "category" "ma_cap